BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:P4m7H9u8fecETjE3Uzx9x
SUMMARY:Programmable control of small non-coding RNA expression in mammalia
	n cells with synthetic RNA polymerase III transactivators.
DTSTAMP:20260705T141835Z
DTSTART:20260710T200000Z
DESCRIPTION:&#160\;  Speaker: Matt Demelo  Doctoral Candidate  Thesis Defen
	se  Department: BioSciences-Biochemistry and Cell Biology  Location: Keck 
	Hall 102  Reliable control of gene expression via synthetic genetic circui
	ts is essential to develop next-generation therapies\, to facilitate effic
	ient bioindustrial processes\, and to enable advanced methods in basic cel
	l biology. Synthetic control of small non-coding RNAs (sncRNAs) is of imme
	nse interest due to their important roles in development and disease in ma
	mmals\, as well as their central role in technologies such as CRISPR/Cas g
	ene editing and RNA interference. RNA polymerase III (RNAP3) is the canoni
	cal polymerase for sncRNA expression\, but few tools for regulated RNAP3 e
	xpression exist\, so genetic circuits requiring regulated sncRNA expressio
	n must use RNA polymerase II (RNAP2) promoters. While there has been exten
	sive development of synthetic regulatory methods for expressing protein-co
	ding RNAs and large non-coding RNAs via RNAP2\, these methods are often in
	effective for robust expression of functional sncRNAs and often require po
	st-transcriptional processing for sncRNAs to achieve functionality\, impai
	ring the feasibility of translating sncRNA technologies into medical and i
	ndustrial applications. Here\, we develop a toolkit capable of highly prog
	rammable and multiplexable expression of synthetic sncRNAs using Synthetic
	 RNAP3 Transcription Factors (Syn3TFs)\, achieving greater than 10-fold im
	provement in efficiency compared to analogous RNAP2-mediated methods. Addi
	tionally\, we outline concise design rules for the construction of minimal
	 RNAP3 promoters and complementary Syn3TFs\, facilitating seamless constru
	ction of orthogonal RNAP3 circuits with customizable outputs. Finally\, we
	 demonstrate the utility of Syn3TFs in performing complex genetic logic an
	d acting as efficient sensors for continuous molecular recording of cellul
	ar events. This platform represents a crucial first step to expanding the 
	arsenal of circuit construction strategies for sncRNA expression and provi
	des a strong foundation for continued development of RNAP3-specific synthe
	tic regulators.  &#160\;  (Department : BioSciences-Biochemistry and Cell 
	Biology)\n\nhttps://events.rice.edu/live/events/433340-programmable-contro
	l-of-small-non-coding-rna\n\nFrom Rice University - Open to the Public
URL:https://events.rice.edu/live/events/433340-programmable-control-of-smal
	l-non-coding-rna
GEO:29.7174;-95.4018
LOCATION:Keck Hall
X-CALRIPPER-SOURCE:rice-public-events
CATEGORIES:Rice University - Open to the Public
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT1H
END:VEVENT
